\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Root order and positive weights}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{The [Fourier-orthogonality hypothesis](fdip-00NI) is
stronger than multiplicative order. In #{R=\mathbb C\times\mathbb C},
the element #{\zeta=(e^{2\pi i/N},1)} has order #{N},
but its nontrivial character sum is
##{\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}\zeta^x=(0,N)\ne(0,0).}
The diagonal choice #{(e^{2\pi i/N},e^{2\pi i/N})} does
satisfy every required root sum, so rings with zero divisors
are included in \ref{fdip-00NJ}.}

\p{For #{N=2}, #{R=\mathbb R} and #{\zeta=-1}, the
strictly positive weights #{w(0)=1}, #{w(1)=2} give
##{\widehat w(0)=\tfrac32,\qquad
 \widehat w(1)=-\tfrac12.}
Their inverse values are #{3/2-1/2=1} and
#{3/2+1/2=2}. Fourier inversion preserves the partition
identity without asserting positivity of transformed
weights. A general weight ring carries no specified order.}
